=== DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: pc1
diagnosis_text:
The fault lies on pc1, not on router2 as the maintenance team believed. Router2's
eth0 is configured as 1.0.0.1/8, which exactly matches its startup script — it
was not modified during maintenance. pc1, however, has been changed at runtime in
a way that diverges from its own startup script.

pc1's startup script (/hostlab/pc1.startup) specifies:
  ip addr add 1.0.0.5/8 dev eth0
  ip route add default via 1.0.0.1

The live system shows 1.0.0.5/30 on eth0 and no default route whatsoever. This
produces two simultaneous failures: (1) with a /30 mask, pc1's directly-connected
subnet is 1.0.0.4/30 (hosts .5 and .6 only), so the gateway 1.0.0.1 falls outside
that range and is unreachable as a neighbor; (2) without a default route, pc1 has
no fallback path to forward packets destined for anything off-link, including the
gateway. Even when router2 attempts to reach pc1 (it sees 1.0.0.5 as part of its
own 1.0.0.0/8 subnet), pc1 cannot route an ICMP reply back toward 1.0.0.1 and
drops it silently — so connectivity is broken in both directions. All other LAN
clients in the topology (pc0, it_hod, etc.) use the correct /24 or /8 masks with
working default routes.

Root cause: During the maintenance window, someone ran `ip addr` and `ip route`
commands on pc1 that replaced its /8 mask with /30 and deleted its default route,
leaving the host isolated on a stub /30 with no gateway.

Corrective fix: Remove the incorrect address and restore the original configuration
on pc1:
  ip addr del 1.0.0.5/30 dev eth0
  ip addr add 1.0.0.5/8 dev eth0
  ip route add default via 1.0.0.1
=== END ===
```